<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Tests\Integration;

use Pramnos\Testing\TestClient;
use Tests\BaseTestCase;

/**
 * Every screen this application serves renders without a fatal.
 *
 * ## What this is for
 *
 * A controller is easy to test; a *rendered view* is not, and that is where a project's
 * uncovered code lives. A scaffolded project starts around 78% coverage and almost all of
 * the gap is views and the controller actions that render them — a 600-statement admin
 * `view.html.php` at 0% is a normal finding.
 *
 * The failures this catches are not subtle ones. They are an entire admin area answering
 * 404 because a config block was never written, a view in a directory nothing reads, a
 * renamed column printing nothing, a fatal on a branch only a populated list reaches. Each
 * of them renders perfectly in the one screen somebody happened to open.
 *
 * ## The two rules that make it safe
 *
 * **An allowlist of read verbs, never a denylist.** A GET to `delete/5` on a controller
 * that does not check the request method deletes row 5. "Most controllers check" is not
 * something to bet a suite on, and the list below is the one place to look.
 *
 * **Seed one row in every table a screen lists.** Without it every list renders its empty
 * state, which is the half of a template nobody gets wrong — the row loop is where a
 * renamed column starts printing nothing and a null blows up a formatter. {@see seedRows()}
 * is where this application says what to seed; it is the only part of this file that is
 * not generic.
 *
 * ## What it asserts
 *
 * Under 500, and not 404 outside {@see DELIBERATE_404}. A 403 is a pass: an action that
 * refuses an unauthenticated sweep is doing its job, and the point here is that the code
 * *ran* rather than that it let us in. Sign in first (see {@see actingAsAdmin()}) to reach
 * further.
 *
 * And that it swept something at all. A discovery that silently returns nothing passes for
 * ever while covering nothing, which is the failure a sweep is most prone to.
 */
class ScreenSweepTest extends BaseTestCase
{
    /**
     * The actions this sweep is allowed to request.
     *
     * Read verbs only, by name, and extend it deliberately. Anything not here is not
     * swept — including `save`, `delete`, `revoke` and every other verb that changes
     * something, whether or not the controller checks the request method.
     *
     * @var list<string>
     */
    private const READ_ACTIONS = [
        'display', 'view', 'edit', 'types', 'data', 'index', 'list',
        'profile', 'security', 'privacy', 'applications', 'exportdata',
        'deleteaccount', 'changepassword', 'notify', 'signinalerts',
    ];

    /**
     * Addresses that answer 404 by design, with the reason.
     *
     * Written down rather than inferred, so "this 404 is fine" is a decision somebody made
     * once and not a rule that quietly swallows the next real one. An email-tracking
     * endpoint redeems a token and 404s without one: there, the 404 *is* the feature.
     *
     * @var array<string, string>
     */
    private const DELIBERATE_404 = [
        // '/Track/open' => 'redeems a one-time token; a 404 without one is correct',
    ];

    /**
     * One row in each table a swept screen lists.
     *
     * **This is the part to fill in.** An empty list renders the empty state, and the
     * empty state is the half of a template that is always right. The row loop — the
     * column names, the formatters, the null handling — only runs when there is a row.
     *
     * Insert with the query builder and let `BaseTestCase` roll the transaction back, or
     * clean up in `tearDown()`. Keep it to one row per table: the sweep is about whether
     * the loop runs, not about pagination.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    protected function seedRows(): void
    {
        // Example:
        // $this->db()->queryBuilder()->table('things')->insert([
        //     'name' => 'A thing', 'created_at' => date('Y-m-d H:i:s'),
        // ]);
    }

    /**
     * One row in each of the **framework's** own tables, for the framework's own screens.
     *
     * Called before {@see seedRows()}, and an application should not have to touch it.
     *
     * ## Why this is here rather than in your project
     *
     * The single largest uncovered file in a project on this framework is usually
     * `Admin/Views/users/view.html.php` — the person card — and it is the framework's
     * file, behind the framework's controller, drawing from seventeen of the framework's
     * tables. One project measured it at 355 of 605 statements after adopting this sweep,
     * a quarter of everything uncovered anywhere.
     *
     * Leaving that to each application means every project on this framework spends the
     * same afternoon working out, table by table and required column by required column,
     * how to make somebody else's biggest view run — or does not, and carries a
     * six-hundred-line file at 58%. Seeding it here took that project to 495 of 605 and
     * its total from 93.49% to 95.30%.
     *
     * ## How it is written
     *
     * **Each insert is absorbed on its own.** A table that is not there is a feature this
     * installation does not have — `authserver`, push notifications, the mail log — and
     * that is not a failure of the sweep. The same rule the framework's own
     * `userRecords()` applies.
     *
     * **Anything that looks unique gets a random value.** `passkey_credentials.credential_id`
     * is unique across the table, so a fixed value seeds the first test that runs and
     * silently seeds nothing in every one after it: the panel renders once and its empty
     * state the rest of the time, which looks exactly like a test that passes.
     *
     * **Required columns nobody named are filled by type.** These rows exist so a row
     * loop executes, not so a screen reads correctly, and a `NOT NULL` column with no
     * default would otherwise refuse the insert on one engine and not the other.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    protected function seedFrameworkRows(): void
    {
        $userId = $this->seededUserId();
        if ($userId === 0) {
            return;
        }

        $now     = date('Y-m-d H:i:s');
        $unique  = static fn(int $bytes = 16): string => bin2hex(random_bytes($bytes));
        $seconds = time();

        $rows = [
            'authserver.user_activity_log'     => ['userid' => $userId, 'action' => 'login', 'ip_address' => '198.51.100.10', 'created_at' => $now],
            'authserver.gdpr_requests'         => ['userid' => $userId, 'request_type' => 'export', 'status' => 'completed', 'requested_at' => $now],
            'authserver.user_privacy_settings' => ['userid' => $userId],
            'authserver.user_twofactor'        => ['userid' => $userId],
            'authserver.passkey_credentials'   => [
                // Unique across the table. A literal here seeds the first test and
                // nothing afterwards — see the note above.
                'credential_id' => $unique(32),
                'userid'        => $userId,
                'public_key'    => $unique(32),
                'name'          => 'A security key',
                'created_at'    => $now,
            ],
            'loginlockouts'                    => ['userid' => $userId],
            'usertokens'                       => [
                'userid'  => $userId,
                'token'   => $unique(),
                'created' => $seconds,
                'lastused' => $seconds,
                'status'  => 1,
            ],
            'tokenactions'                     => ['servertime' => $seconds],
            'mails'                            => ['userid' => $userId, 'subject' => 'A message', 'senddate' => $seconds],
            'pramnos.pushsubscriptions'        => ['userid' => $userId, 'endpoint' => 'https://example.com/push/' . $unique(8)],
            'pramnos.pushlog'                  => ['userid' => $userId, 'title' => 'A notification'],
        ];

        foreach ($rows as $table => $values) {
            $this->seedOne($table, $values);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Insert one row, filling whatever else the table insists on, and absorb a refusal.
     *
     * @param array<string, mixed> $values
     */
    private function seedOne(string $table, array $values): void
    {
        $db = \Pramnos\Framework\Factory::getDatabase();

        try {
            if (!$db->schema()->hasTable($table)) {
                // A feature this installation does not have.
                return;
            }

            // One shape on both engines. `getColumns()` answers what the driver
            // answers, and the two disagree on every key this loop needs: the default is
            // `COLUMN_DEFAULT` on MySQL and `column_default` on PostgreSQL, the primary
            // key is `Key = PRI` on one and `PrimaryKey = true` on the other. Reading one
            // spelling works on one engine and silently does nothing on the other,
            // because a missing array key is `null` and `null` reads as "no default".
            $present = $db->schema()->columnDetails($table);
            if ($present === []) {
                return;
            }

            $row = [];
            foreach ($values as $name => $value) {
                // A column this installation's version of the table does not have is
                // dropped rather than refused: the seed should survive a schema that
                // moved on.
                if (isset($present[$name])) {
                    $row[$name] = $value;
                }
            }

            foreach ($present as $name => $column) {
                if (array_key_exists($name, $row) || $column['nullable']) {
                    continue;
                }

                /*
                 * Two columns are never invented for, and both were, which cost four of
                 * the seven panels this method exists to render:
                 *
                 *  - **anything with a default.** A `bigserial` primary key reports
                 *    `nextval('…')`, not auto-increment, so an integer placeholder went
                 *    into it and collided with every row a week of testing had left —
                 *    `duplicate key value violates "passkey_credentials_pkey"`.
                 *  - **a foreign key.** `tokenactions.urlid` points into the route table,
                 *    and a value invented for it cannot point anywhere:
                 *    `violates foreign key constraint "fk_tokenactions_urlid"`.
                 *
                 * Each refusal was absorbed, correctly, so the sweep stayed green and the
                 * person card rendered four fewer panels than it could — which looks
                 * exactly like the feature working.
                 *
                 * The second is the line worth keeping: a row so the loop runs is the
                 * point; a row that claims something untrue about another table is not.
                 */
                if ($column['hasDefault'] || $column['isPrimary'] || $column['isForeign']) {
                    continue;
                }

                $row[$name] = $this->placeholderFor($column['type']);
            }

            if ($row === []) {
                return;
            }

            $db->queryBuilder()->table($table)->insert($row);
        } catch (\Throwable) {
            /*
             * One row in one table of a seeder. A refusal here must not fail the sweep:
             * what the sweep is for is the screens, and a screen whose panel renders its
             * empty state is still a screen that rendered.
             */
        }
    }

    /**
     * Something a NOT NULL column of this type will accept.
     *
     * The type arrives already lower-cased and without its length, from
     * `SchemaBuilder::columnDetails()`.
     */
    private function placeholderFor(string $type): mixed
    {
        return match (true) {
            str_contains($type, 'int')       => 0,
            str_contains($type, 'bool')      => 0,
            str_contains($type, 'decimal'),
            str_contains($type, 'numeric'),
            str_contains($type, 'float'),
            str_contains($type, 'double')    => 0,
            str_contains($type, 'timestamp'),
            str_contains($type, 'datetime')  => date('Y-m-d H:i:s'),
            str_contains($type, 'date')      => date('Y-m-d'),
            default                          => '',
        };
    }

    /**
     * The id of a person the framework's own screens can be swept against.
     *
     * The signed-in account when {@see actingAsAdmin()} left one, otherwise the lowest
     * real user id in the table. Zero when there is nobody at all, in which case the
     * framework rows are not seeded — a person card with no person is not a screen.
     */
    protected function seededUserId(): int
    {
        $current = \Pramnos\User\User::getCurrentUser();
        if ($current !== null && (int) ($current->userid ?? 0) > 1) {
            return (int) $current->userid;
        }

        try {
            $result = \Pramnos\Framework\Factory::getDatabase()->queryBuilder()
                ->table('#PREFIX#users')
                ->select(['userid'])
                ->where('userid', 1, '>')
                ->orderBy('userid', 'asc')
                ->first();
        } catch (\Throwable) {
            return 0;
        }

        return (int) ($result->fields['userid'] ?? 0);
    }

    /**
     * The id to append for one action, when the screen is about a subject.
     *
     * **Fill this in, or half the sweep measures nothing.** `/admin/Users/view` with no
     * id is a valid request that answers 200 — and what renders is the "nothing
     * selected" branch: four lines at the top of a template whose six hundred are the
     * point. The sweep passes, and the number it produces comes entirely from the half
     * of the file it never reaches. Adopting the sweep without this made one project's
     * coverage *fall* from 74.8% to 45.0%.
     *
     * It is the same failure `seedRows()` is about, one layer along. A list with no rows
     * renders its empty state; a detail view with no subject renders its empty state
     * too, and there is no row to seed because what is missing is not data but an id in
     * the URL.
     *
     * **A `match` on the action, not one shared id.** Handing `view` a channel id where
     * it wants a user id produces a screen that renders its "not found" branch and a
     * sweep that reports success over it — the same bug again, one layer further down.
     *
     * ```php
     * protected function subjectFor(string $prefix, string $action): string
     * {
     *     return match ($action) {
     *         'view', 'edit'  => (string) $this->seededUserId,
     *         'log'           => (string) $this->seededChannelId,
     *         default         => '',
     *     };
     * }
     * ```
     *
     * `seedRows()` is where those ids are kept. Returning `''` — the default — requests
     * the action with no subject, which is where a project that overrides neither is
     * today.
     *
     * @param  string $prefix The URL prefix the controller is under (`''` or `/admin`)
     * @param  string $action The action about to be requested
     * @return string The id to append, or `''` for none
     */
    protected function subjectFor(string $prefix, string $action): string
    {
        return '';
    }

    /**
     * Sign in, when the screens worth sweeping are behind a login.
     *
     * Left to the application because "an administrator" is a product decision. Without
     * it the sweep still runs and still catches a fatal — an action that refuses is code
     * that ran — but it stops at the first guard and covers the refusal rather than the
     * screen.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    protected function actingAsAdmin(): void
    {
    }

    protected function setUp(): void
    {
        parent::setUp();

        $this->actingAsAdmin();

        // The framework's tables first: `seedRows()` may depend on a person existing,
        // and this is where one is found.
        $this->seedFrameworkRows();
        $this->seedRows();
    }

    /**
     * Every controller answers its default action.
     *
     * The cheapest half of the sweep and the one that found an entire admin area
     * answering 404: `init` had written no `admin` block, so every screen in it was
     * missing and every one of them looked like a routing mistake in the application.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    public function testEveryControllerAnswersItsDefaultAction(): void
    {
        // Arrange
        $controllers = $this->controllers();
        $this->assertNotEmpty($controllers, 'no controllers were discovered, so this sweeps nothing');

        // Act + Assert
        $failures = [];
        foreach ($controllers as $prefix => $names) {
            foreach ($names as $name) {
                $failure = $this->request($prefix . '/' . $name);
                if ($failure !== null) {
                    $failures[] = $failure;
                }
            }
        }

        $this->assertSame([], $failures, "Screens that did not answer:\n" . implode("\n", $failures));
    }

    /**
     * And every read action on every controller.
     *
     * Where the rendering is. A controller's `display()` is usually a list, which is a
     * table and a loop; the long view nobody has opened is `view`, and the form with
     * thirty fields is `edit`.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    public function testEveryReadActionRenders(): void
    {
        // Arrange
        $swept    = 0;
        $failures = [];

        // Act
        foreach ($this->controllers() as $prefix => $names) {
            foreach ($names as $name) {
                foreach ($this->readActionsOf($prefix, $name) as $action) {
                    $swept++;

                    // The subject, where the screen is about one. Without it `view` and
                    // `edit` render their "nothing selected" branch and the sweep covers
                    // four lines of a six-hundred-line template.
                    $subject = $this->subjectFor($prefix, $action);
                    $path    = $prefix . '/' . $name . '/' . $action
                        . ($subject === '' ? '' : '/' . rawurlencode($subject));

                    $failure = $this->request($path);
                    if ($failure !== null) {
                        $failures[] = $failure;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // Assert — that it swept something, first: a discovery returning nothing passes
        // for ever while covering nothing.
        $this->assertGreaterThan(0, $swept, 'no read actions were discovered');
        $this->assertSame([], $failures, "Screens that did not answer:\n" . implode("\n", $failures));
    }

    /**
     * One request, and what was wrong with it — or null.
     *
     * A 403 is a pass. The action ran and refused, which is the code being exercised; an
     * anonymous sweep that demanded 200 everywhere would be asserting that nothing is
     * protected.
     */
    private function request(string $path): ?string
    {
        try {
            $status = (new TestClient())->get($path)->getResponse()->getStatusCode();
        } catch (\Throwable $ex) {
            return $path . ' — ' . get_class($ex) . ': ' . $ex->getMessage();
        }

        if ($status >= 500) {
            return $path . ' — ' . $status;
        }

        if ($status === 404 && !array_key_exists($path, self::DELIBERATE_404)) {
            return $path . ' — 404 (add it to DELIBERATE_404 with a reason if that is correct)';
        }

        return null;
    }

    /**
     * The application's controllers, by URL prefix.
     *
     * Read from the filesystem rather than from a list, so a controller added next month
     * is swept without anybody remembering to add it — which is the whole value.
     *
     * @return array<string, list<string>> prefix => controller names
     */
    private function controllers(): array
    {
        $found = [];

        foreach (['' => 'src/Controllers', '/{{ adminPrefix }}' => 'src/Admin/Controllers'] as $prefix => $directory) {
            $path = ROOT . '/' . $directory;
            if (!is_dir($path)) {
                continue;
            }

            $names = [];
            foreach (glob($path . '/*.php') ?: [] as $file) {
                $names[] = basename($file, '.php');
            }

            if ($names !== []) {
                sort($names);
                $found[$prefix] = $names;
            }
        }

        return $found;
    }

    /**
     * The read actions a controller actually declares.
     *
     * Reflection over its public methods, intersected with the allowlist — so a
     * controller that has no `edit` is not requested for one, and a 404 in the results is
     * a real missing screen rather than an action that was never written.
     *
     * @return list<string>
     */
    private function readActionsOf(string $prefix, string $name): array
    {
        $class = $prefix === ''
            ? '\\{{ namespace }}\\Controllers\\' . $name
            : '\\{{ namespace }}\\Admin\\Controllers\\' . $name;

        if (!class_exists($class)) {
            return [];
        }

        $actions = [];
        foreach ((new \ReflectionClass($class))->getMethods(\ReflectionMethod::IS_PUBLIC) as $method) {
            $lower = strtolower($method->getName());
            if ($lower === 'display') {
                // Covered by the default-action sweep above; requesting it twice only
                // doubles the time.
                continue;
            }
            if (in_array($lower, self::READ_ACTIONS, true)) {
                $actions[] = $lower;
            }
        }

        sort($actions);

        return $actions;
    }
}
